any of your colleagues in the  Conservative Party take your view having  it's quite a sizable number actually in  the parliamentary party and in the party  at at large I'm not going to put a  number on it because it's very much a  moving feast at the moment we obviously  had some clarification from the Prime  Minister last weekend about the timing  of the triggering of article 50 this  idea of having a great repeal bill but  clearly the the big issue is going to be  the terms on which we leave the EU and  the terms of our continuing relationship  with EU member states but just let me  ask you about you know the number of I  also bet those that think this way but  the number who feel free to speak out  it's almost as if the remains have  disappeared within the Conservative  Party we know they were the majority  people like you people like Hannah sue  berry are speaking out but many have  headed to the hills  amber Rudd I mean she's not that  secretary bound by collective  responsibility but she was have vocal  remain supporter yet she came out with  that speech were you horrified at  conference well I was I was very  surprised that amber had put forward  that proposal about the listing of  foreign workers because that's not the  amber Rudd that I know and I worked  alongside in the in the cabinet and I  think it's on those things I mean  incidentally she was saying and others  saying it's a consultation but the  trouble with these sorts of policies is  that they send out a message about the  the party about the way that we want to  approach people coming to this country  bringing their talents their skills here  and I do think as a message that we want  to send out to to the wider world and to  reduce the word repugnant and that's  used by Steve Hilton former adviser to  then Prime Minister David Cameron well I  think it's it is I probably would  actually I mean I think it's it's a  really inadvisable way to proceed I mean  there's no doubt people do care in this  country about the levels of immigration  and I think that we've moved from  position where in 2005 where Michael had  was part leader in that election he  talked to immigration and that debate  was shut down by the then Labour  government but we appear to have now  gone through almost you know 180 degrees  the other way I suppose which is that  actually now where we're now saying  we're going to open ocean shut the  borders we don't want people to come  here and I think that's also  we want to see as a country I think  Steve Hilton has a point this morning  and he writes about having an open  country about people coming here look I  represent a constituency with a large  international university and they would  be a huge disadvantage if they didn't  have academics coming here if they  didn't have students coming here you  know we saw the disturbing development  on Friday of academics at the LSE being  told unless you have a British passport  you know we don't want to have you  advising the UK government and that also  seems a strange development to me - and  another one picking out from the  conference Jeremy Hunt another remainder  of course coming out with extraordinary  idea that somehow the NHS will become he  Trump's self-sufficient in in pretty  staffing in ten years time it's  presumably not achievable we surprised  he said something like that well I  wasn't entirely surprised because there  has been a debate around the cabinet  table about obviously in training  medical students is a huge investment  for this country and I think we should  ask ourselves why is that the so many  having been trained then decide  immediately to to go overseas so what  can we do to incentivize people to to  stay here but again you see the message  you can give is well actually we only  want British doctors we don't want  foreign expertise coming here and it's  varying listen to some interviews given  by people working in the health service  over the last couple of days saying  actually our health service is stronger  because we do have people from overseas  and also our doctors go overseas again  to build up their experience which they  can then bring back which benefits  patients here so I think all of those  those signals are it's quite it's it's  rather confusing  when actually what we want to be  focusing on is how do we give that  messages there about being in an open  country that welcomes all talents to  come here but we want do the best thing  whether it's for our public services  whether it's for our businesses and  other organisations and making sure  they've got access to the best talents  possible so you know these questions  come in and what can you do do you feel  to influence the course of events for  instance can you get some kind of  parliamentary vote on the process  perhaps further down the line and not  just on this great repeal bill do you  think you can make common cause with  people in other parties to do that well  it there's no doubt that there's a lot  of us in  Parliament who do you feel that it would  be extraordinary given that the brexit  vote was about the sovereignty of  Parliament and of this country in terms  of making our laws that phrase of taking  back control whatever it means for  Parliament not to have a big say in the  brexit negotiations as they unfold on  the guiding principles to be asking  questions yeah I hope tomorrow for  example that you know a member of the  cabinet whether it's David Davis or  somebody else will come to Parliament  and make a statement that's what  normally happens when Parliament hasn't  been sitting when there been some big  developments as there were over the  party conference the announcements  coming to Parliament to tell Parliament  this is the stage that we have got to  and yes I think there will be common  cause between those of us who want there  to be the right brexit the right  mechanism for leaving the EU but the  other thing is that the Conservative  Party manifesto on which we were elected  only seventeen months ago has a very  clear statement in it about we say yes  to the single market and it talks about  we will safeguard British interests in  the single market and that's something  that the government cannot ignore the  Conservative Party my conservative  colleagues cannot ignore we stood on a  manifesto saying that as well as saying  we'd have a referendum we would respect  the result which we will but actually we  have to make sure that we do not throw  away access the single market just  because that means talking about free  movement so further down the line as a  deal comes in to shape you would like to  see Parliament retaking sovereignty  Parliament having a decision well that's  I think what everybody seems to be  saying campaigners on the lee side the  Prime Minister was talking about  sovereignty of Parliament upon making  their own the laws and having a say in  this negotiation and you know and I  think boat and I think we as members of  parliament are very mindful even those  of us who are very Pro remain are very  mindful of the vote on the 23rd of June  what people wanted the majority  seventeen million have voted to leave  the EU but there is a 48 percent as a 16  million of whom I've had a lot of  contact from in the last few days saying  you know please make sure you make the  case for the best possible deal and have  you had contacts from the former Labour  leader Ed Miliband we hear he's  yeah boarding the papers there he's  given the Conservatives know what I  haven't well I look I will sir I will  speak to to to everyone and because I  think this is such a momentous issue for  the United Kingdom I think we can all  agree on that whatever side of the  debate that we on this is a huge thing  for the United Kingdom it is going to  affect our future for decades to come  which is why we've got to get it right  and I think people expect their members  of parliament to be holding the  government to account to be asking those  questions and isn't it interesting that  actually you've got people like pat  you've got people whether it's Ed  Miliband or others it's not the labor  front bench who are asking those  questions so it's incumbent on others of  us to do that you must be getting a lot  of flack from some members of your own  party actually I'll say no and then I'll  probably look at my Twitter timeline and  look at my inbox after after that but I  think people understand that it's  incumbent on those of us but it is  who've been in government who understand  how government works to ask those  constructive questions about do we have  the right deal what are the negotiating  principles where are we hoping to end up  now what damage could we do to our  economy what damage can we avoid doing  to our economy and that's what we will  be asking do you think I mean there is a  school of thought that says with treasom  a that this was positioning at the  Conservative Party conference she after  all was I remain err maybe technically a  remainder but she had to then reassure  the party that she was going to carry  out brexit and having reassured them  she now has room for manoeuvre there are  going to be compromises inevitably in  these very very difficult negotiations  do you think that its political tactics  well I'm sure there's an element of that  I mean I think that in any party  conference speech you give you a think  about the audience that's in front of  you the reassurance is that people  wanted to hear but I don't think anyone  can doubt from what Theresa May has said  from July onwards that she is determined  to deliver brexit we surprised she took  this such a strong tank though not  particularly because I think at the  start of a negotiation you want to set  out your you know your your your stall  but I think other people clearly have  been look at the markets look at what's  happened to the value of the pound in  the last few days I think there are  people 